 might say was hubris but I think  also he looked at the electorate and at  the time he looked at the conservative  party in parliament one of the  interesting things about this referendum  is that in the Conservative Party itself  has 330 members of parliament more than  180 came out in favor of referent of  remaining before the referendum so there  was a clear majority in his party and a  even larger majority in the parliament  in favor of staying in right right I  have to say you know I watched that  voting very closely myself because you  know virtually everyone was saying that  it was not going to happen that they  would not vote the UK out of the EU so  it is i think it's mind-boggling that  the people did vote to do so and and  after the election there is talk about  people not understanding what they were  voting for which i find you know  somewhat disconcerting because now  you're in a situation that you probably  don't want to be in I mean is there a  way can they have a vote to vote again  they could if the government was willing  to have a second referendum but Theresa  May has said there won't be a second  referendum the first thing she said when  she became the leader of the  Conservative Party back in july was  brexit means brexit and that's very  straightforward you might say also it's  not only concise but it's circular  nevertheless  durex it means braixen and so from her  point of view and the government's point  of view even though two-thirds of the  members of the cabinet are people who  supported staying in this government is  going to try to exit right right you  know what I was getting at is because if  the people truly didn't understand what  they were voting for does it need to be  looked at again if that's the case well  it probably should be and what's  interesting is that although she has  said no to a second referendum a number  of people have argued that at the least  there ought to be a vote in parliament  before Theresa May acts on the process  starts the process of exiting and that's  now before the courts in in the UK and  ultimately it will go to the Supreme  Court and do you have any idea what way  that will go it's hard to say it's hard  to say the the problem is there is a  there's a process that the EU has for  withdrawal in which member state has to  notify the European Council of its  intention to withdraw she said at the  conservative party conference that the  government would do that no later than  March of 2017 at that point there are  two years the clock starts ticking there  are two years in which to negotiate a  withdrawal agreement if that agreement  takes effect within two years the UK is  out but if there's no agreement and  unless the entire membership of the  European Council agrees to extend the  negotiation the UK is out without a  withdrawal agreement in what would be  called a the hardest possible brexit  meaning no agreement on the relationship  half  the exit okay it seems like it's I mean  this has never happened before with any  other country so it's you know it's it's  a first time occurrence and I would  imagine that there's a lot of questions  that are involved in the process of how  you move forward with this I mean have  you read the documents that actually  explain how this gets taken care of well  what have your complicated do you mean  in the negotiation at this point I mean  for the whole thing I mean well there is  there is a clearly stated process for  for what will happen next at least  procedurally in the treaty of lisbon in  2007 the EU for the first time provided  this process for a withdrawal so we know  what will happen is some time after  march 2017 the EU and the UK will  negotiate and the UK will try to get  what it thinks will be the best possible  deal for itself and the EU will  negotiate with its concerns which  evolved involve among other things  issues such as if we give the UK  continued access to our internal market  for their auto exporters for their  financial services firms will the UK in  exchange accept our rules and  regulations the jurisdiction of the  European Court of Justice will they  continue to apply the principle of free  movement meaning European Union citizens  can continue to move to the UK without  limitations the problem of course is  that there are a number of interests in  the UK that would like to retain the  access to the internal market and the  single market auto manufacturers  Japanese auto manufacturers the City of  London  there's huge money involved in Scotland  there are many interests that want to  stay in the single market but the quid  for the quo is you have to accept free  movement you have to accept regulations  and rules by the EU you have to also  make a contribution to the EU budget and  wouldn't that be just like still being  in the it would be almost that would be  that would be a very soft brexit that  that's more or less what what people are  thinking of when they think of soft  brexit you're out of the EU but you're  in a relationship where you still have  the access to the internal market this  is something that is there Norway  Iceland and Liechtenstein all have this  kind of arrangement the problem is it  means for the British they have to  accept free movement and of course we  know immigration was a huge issue in the  referendum they have to continue to  accept the rules and regulations of the  European institutions and that was a big  issue for many who thought that  Parliament had to regain its sovereignty  they have to accept the court of justice  and they have to pay money so now the  first real thing is this negotiation  between the UK and the EU right that I  think will be very interesting and what  are your thoughts on that well it'll be  very interesting because the the  especially interesting thing in this of  course is that because of the two-year  clock the EU could just sit on its hands  for two years let the clock tick and and  the UK would be faced with an exit in  March 2019 without anything without any  deal at all right oh and what do you  think so they have a lot of leverage the  EU has a lot of leverage in this most of  the discussion right now has been about  what does the UK wat and of course  there's no consensus yet in the  government about what they do want  there's a lot of argument about that  the politicians of the UK pretty much  wanted to stay it's not until they let  it open to the people that it became  this this problem so it's a very  interesting situation I think well it  raises a raises an interesting question  about the role of a referendum because  the question was given to the voters was  do you want to remain or leave and it  was really that simple and there was no  real elaboration of what the  consequences of leave would be and as a  result a lot of voters reacted in a  visceral way and reflecting a  long-standing ambivalence in the UK  about he are they in are they really  part of Europe or not and it was easy to  say leave but the consequences of that  are pretty a quite dire all right I  think also with the people voting it was  really at a time when immigration the  refugee crisis was is was huge and I  think that really played in as well too  in the voters minds I'm sure it did yeah  there there has been an increase of  immigration from EU countries in recent  years since 2012 especially from Central  and Eastern European member states  Poland Romania the largest increases in  the to the UK and so that and that and  in the context of what's been happening  with Syria and the refugee flows into  Europe has really put immigration on the  front burner for the European public as  well as the British public okay so let's  talk a little bit about Theresa May  she's virtually unknown I think in this  country to us you know give us an  overview of her and and how you think  she will broker this deal moving forward  well she's been in parliament since 1997  before that she worked for 20 years in  some of the time for the Bank of England  and in financial services she represents  a constituency to the west of London she  has spent the last six years before  becoming Prime Minister as Home  Secretary which is a very important  powerful position in the cabinet in  charge of policing immigration  ironically immigration home affairs she  was a you might say a quiet supporter of  the remaining she wasn't a outspoken  advocate but she was supporting remain  she's by all accounts a really good  administrator and leader and and will be  an effective Prime Minister for sure and  she's got an interesting government  Boris Johnson is the foreign secretary  who is something of a loose cannon and  there are other important Brett suitors  in the cabinet they've created a new  department in the government called the  department for exiting the EU not a very  not a terrific name for department but  that tells what it's in charge of at  least know what it is and she now has  the job of trying to put together the  position that the UK will want to  advocate in its negotiations and she  will be in charge of that herself there  will be a cabinet committee that she  chairs that will be in charge of the  negotiation on the UK side so I mean  it's sounding like the UK wants a soft  exit would be my guess i would say i  would say there are very strong economic  interest most notably in the city of  london  financial services where they have well  over 300,000 jobs in firms that are  deeply involved in all aspects of  finance in the EU but I think what we're  going to see is some I think what is  taking shape is something that is  neither hardened or soft and she's made  a point of saying we're not doing a hard  exit or a soft exit Allah Norway we're  going to do our own unique deal a  flexible brexit is the way some people  have thought about it where they what  they want to do is is they want free  unimpeded access to the internal market  for certain important sectors finance  auto exporters so forth so they want  essentially all the power rights to  enter the market on the other hand they  also want full control over immigration  they don't want to be under the european  court of justice and the question is can  they get that well can I was going to  say and where is the you the EU coming  down on on their side I mean are they  upset number one that the referendum was  even held I think I think they're upset  about it because it means they've got to  deal with brexit on top of everything  else they're dealing with they're still  dealing with Greece sure I mean you and  I have talked before about grace and the  eurozone crisis that's not over yet they  have the refugee crisis from Syria which  is really the overwhelming issue facing  them just recently they've run into  problems with a very large trade deal  they negotiated with Canada that has now  been put on hold because one region of  Belgium has refused to accept the  agreement so they have their hands  full and they have elections coming up  in France in the spring in germany next  fall where leaders such as Hollande and  miracle or will be contesting so the  last thing the last thing they really  need is is brexit so it's sounding like  it's really in the uk's best interest to  try and negotiate a deal as quickly as  possible with the EU because if that  two-year period runs out they're going  to be in trouble there out there out  without anything and exactly which I  don't think is a place that they the UK  wants to be so do you think that the EU  is going to be i'll use the word  punitive and their approach to their  negotiation I don't think they'll be  punitive but I think they'll be very  tough they're not going to give up on  free movement that is that is a red line  for them and they will not give up on  that and that will be a deal-breaker it  will be unless the British figure an  ingenious way to sort of work around  that and and there was there is some  possibility they could come up with  something that it actually is something  that David Cameron negotiated in  February as his deal that led him to say  okay I've renegotiated I got this now  let's have a referendum they might be  able to finesse that issue the British  might be able to finesse that but the  uke the EU is going to be very tough on  its negotiation because the EU market is  much more important for the UK then the  British market is for EU as a whole and  everyone knows that and the EU also has  very powerful leverage in this not only  the two-year clock that will just start  ticking  but also there are 27 member states so  all of them have to be satisfied in this  deal also the european parliament has to  approve a deal so it's their hands are  going to be tied in so many ways that  ultimately it's going to be the case  that i think the EU will decide what the  deal will be and the UK will have to  accept it right and that's how it's  going to go down and then they will have  to tell their voters in May 2020 that  they got the deal that is in the best  interests of the UK but now then there's  an additional wrinkle in all of this of  course which is Scotland ok because  Scotland has made it very clear that  since they voted overwhelmingly to stay  in that they are going to be very  attentive to whether their interests  will be served in whatever deal the UK  negotiates and if not they will hold  another independence referendum and in  fact put forward a bill an independence  referendum bill last week holding that  in reserve in case they don't get the  deal they want so they will separate  from the UK if what they don't get to  stay and what what the First Minister  Nicola Sturgeon of the Scottish National  Party is saying we are going to try to  get our deal the best deal we can get  for Scotland in in the brexit  negotiation but if we don't get that and  if our interests are challenged and hurt  by the brexit nuh goes agreement then we  will go forward with a referendum  because her view is that it's  democratically unacceptable those are  the words she used for Scotland which  voted sixty-two percent in favor  remaining to have to leave the single  market right even if even if the UK  leaves the EU Scotland wants to retain  membership in the single market and that  means soft brexit so there there are  many powerful voices pulling Theresa May  as much as she may resist it toward a  soft brexit solution and my guess would  be at the end of the day that's probably  what we'll get but no one wants to say  that yet because that antagonizes the  break suitors well I think it'll be very  a very interesting process to watch over  the next several months to see how  things play out several months and years  yes thank you David for being here with  us today and I giving us your update on  brexit for more information about  professor Cameron and his work please  visit our website at MIT macmillan  report yale edu be sure to join us again  for another episode of the macmillan  report made possible through funding  from the Whitney and Betty Macmillan  Center for International and area  studies at Yale  [Music]  I

